#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c6b30e7-fba6-597c-8306-27d707bfc244" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting One Exponent"}] :question/id #uuid "baae925b-d23d-56db-b15f-b438f68df608" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-4101" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $3^{3x-3}=9^x$." :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $9$ as ${\\color{red}3}^2$, and then rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^{3x - 3} &= 9^{x} \\\\\n3^{3x - 3} &= (3^{2})^{x} \\\\\n3^{3x - 3} &= 3^{2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3})$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 3 &= 2x \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114965 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8eebff2-97ca-599b-a819-04ff34f0c1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting Two Exponents"}] :question/id #uuid "e73675af-973f-583e-9909-0eb9630fdcc8" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-4161" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $9^{3x}=27^{x+2}$." :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using\n\n$$\n9={\\color{red}3}^2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n27 = {\\color{red}3}^3\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{red}3}^2\\right)^{3x}=\\left({\\color{red}3}^3\\right)^{x+2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n3^{2(3x)} = 3^{3(x + 2)}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3})$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(3x) &= 3(x + 2) \\\\\n6x &= 3x + 6 \\\\\n3x &= 6 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
